WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:01.799
Swamp Thing!

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:02.300
He is amazing!

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.700
And since his debut in 1974, he's been one
of the most fascinating characters in the

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.320
entire DC Comics lineup.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.360
From his origins as a straightforward man-turned-monster,
to his strange luck on the screen, here’s

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.120
what you might not know about DC's resident
muck monster.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.140
Convergent evolution

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.180
While he would definitely go on to become
the most famous, Swamp Thing is by no means

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:23.800
the first swamp monster in comics.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:28.279
In fact, he's not even the first one created
in the early '70s at one of the major superhero

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:28.880
publishers.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.140
That honor in particular goes to Marvel's
Man-Thing, created by Roy Thomas, Gerry Conway,

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.790
and Gray Morrow in the pages of Savage Tales
#1.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:41.420
In that story, Dr. Ted Sallis is working in
the swamp on a formula meant to give people

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.719
super-powers when he's attacked by criminals
and gets exposed to it himself, plunging into

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.040
the swamp and emerging as...

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.040
Man-Thing!

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.340
It was only months later that Len Wein and
Berni Wrightson introduced their new swamp

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.840
monster in the pages of House of Secrets #92.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:59.460
In that story, Dr. Alex Olsen, later changed
to Alec Holland, is working in the swamp on

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.460
a formula meant to give people super-powers
when he's attacked by criminals and gets exposed

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.140
to it himself, plunging into the swamp and
emerging as ... Swamp Thing!

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.640
did I just get deja vu...

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.210
Needless to say, Conway noticed the similarities
between Man Thing and Swamp Thing, which were

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:17.180
especially apparent since he and Wein actually
roommates in New York at the time.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:18.420
The Green

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.900
In 1986, Swamp Thing became something more
than just a monster.

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:26.530
Instead, he was the latest in a long line
of plant elementals who were connected to

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:30.920
what was called the Green a mystical field
encompassing all of Earth's plant life.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.840
From there, Swamp Thing was connected to other
plant monsters: DC characters like the Floronic

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.290
Man, Black Orchid, Solomon Grundy, and even
Poison Ivy were added to that connection,

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.070
but it went beyond just their pages.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:46.410
Another elemental that we get an explicit
backstory for shares a suspiciously similar

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:50.640
origin with a golden age swamp monster called
the Heap, and a reference to the repeated

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.020
pattern of origin stories is pretty easy to
read as an acknowledgement of Man-Thing, too.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.940
The Red, the Clear, and the Rot

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.340
Connecting Swamp Thing to the Green gave DC
an idea that’s been extended to a whole

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.380
lot of other characters, complete with their
own rainbow of colors.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:05.900
Animal Man had a head start.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.880
The idea behind this forgotten hero had always
been that he could access the abilities of

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:14.040
animals by tapping into a "morphogenetic field,”
which later became known as The Red.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.380
It didn't stop there, either.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.040
Aquaman's ability to talk to fish was explained
as the ability to tap into the watery force

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.260
called the Clear, and the same story that
tied Animal Man to the Red also introduced

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.380
the death force, known as the Rot, which was
pretty much bad news for everyone.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.540
Love in the time of dendrophilia

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.960
In the beginning, "Alec Holland" was mourning
both his own status as an undead muck monster

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.780
and the death of his wife, Linda, who perished
during his fiery origin.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.560
Meanwhile, the human perspective on his adventures
fell to Abigail Arcane niece of the depraved

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.820
supervillain Anton Arcane and her boyfriend
Matt Cable.

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:51.060
Unfortunately for her, Matt was killed, and
in one of the most horrifying moments of the

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.160
series, it was revealed that Anton had been
possessing Matt's body without Abby knowing.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.900
Once that was taken care of and Anton was
sent straight to hell, the way was clear for

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.600
Swamp Thing and Abby to fall in love with
each other.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.850
This sparked one of DC's most enduring romances
which is kind of surprising considering that

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.860
one of the lovers in question was a shambling
monster made of trees.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.380
He beat Batman

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.340
You might find this hard to believe, but it
turns out that in some states, it's actually

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:17.150
illegal to… um… how should we say this…
wait.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.460
I got this - "bump uglies" with a swamp monster...
get it?

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:21.020
Ugly.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:21.680
Monster.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.180
Anyway…

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:27.260
When the locals discovered that Abby Arcane
was engaging in dendrophilia, she was arrested,

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.440
and Swamp Thing wasn't happy.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.940
He launched an all out attack on the city
that was holding her… a little place called

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:32.440
Gotham.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.600
Needless to say, Batman didn't appreciate
having his hometown turned into a jungle.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:40.440
While he did his level best to fight the Swamp
Thing off, he found out that an Avatar of

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.240
the Green isn't as easy to take down as, say,
the Riddler.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:47.720
Instead, Swampy just kept coming back bigger
and tougher, until Batman finally saw his

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:48.220
point.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.620
The Dark Knight figured that if you're going
to arrest non-human heroes for having romantic

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.530
relationships with human women, you're eventually
going to have to try slapping handcuffs on

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:56.800
Lois Lane and Superman.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.200
Reefer madness

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.000
Sometimes, it can pay to have a friend with
absolute mastery over plants.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:07.360
In Hellblazer #63, he got an invite to John
Constantine's 40th birthday party, which came

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.220
as a surprise since the characters had never
exactly been pals.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:15.120
Even so, Swamp Thing gave Constantine a birthday
present by rapidly growing a single marijuana

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:16.530
leaf into enough for everyone.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.460
"Communing with the Green" indeed.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.580
Swamp to the Future

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.139
When Rick Veitch took over Swamp Thing in
1987, he launched one of the most memorable

00:04:25.139 --> 00:04:29.550
stories on the book ever: a time-travel epic
that saw Swamp Thing hurled back through the

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:33.430
ages, encountering heroes and villains from
throughout DC Comics history.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:35.740
And then he abruptly left the book in mid-story.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.680
The reason for was no secret, and was even
the topic of discussion in the book's letters

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:44.199
pages for several months: DC asked Veitch at
the last minute to change the end of his storyline,

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:44.840
and he refused.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:45.700
The reason?

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.479
Rick Veitch's original plan had been for Swamp
Thing’s next stop to land him in the Roman

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:54.420
Empire circa 33 AD, where he'd meet an obscure
character called Golden Gladiator.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.600
Oh, and he was also going to team up with
Jesus.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:00.320
Only a few years later, it's unlikely that
this story would've been a problem.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.930
By 1995, DC's Vertigo imprint was publishing
Preacher, a book that made "Swamp Thing meets

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.849
Jesus" look like a Sunday School lesson.

00:05:07.849 --> 00:05:11.640
At the time, though, Swamp Thing was still
firmly set in the DC Universe.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.080
To this day, his version of Swamp Thing #88
remains unpublished.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:18.160
Swamp Thing conquers the screen... sort of

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.610
With critically acclaimed stories and a dedicated
fan base, it makes sense that Warner Bros.

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:24.180
would bring Swamp Thing to the big screen.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:29.100
The first attempt came in 1982 from Wes Craven,
the legendary horror director who went on

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.569
to direct A Nightmare on Elm Street and Scream.

00:05:31.569 --> 00:05:36.000
It stuck to Wein and Wrightson's origin story,
pitting Alec Holland against Anton Arcane,

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:38.080
with Adrienne Barbeau as a love interest.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.760
It wasn't exactly a success, but it garnered
generally positive reviews.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.600
1988’s Return of Swamp Thing wasn't so lucky.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:48.300
With a low budget and a bad script, it flopped
hard at the box office.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:52.360
It did, however, pave the way for not one,
but two television shows.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.819
In 1990, Dick Durock returned as Swamp Thing
for a series that ran for three seasons on

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:57.320
USA.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.140
In 1991, things got even weirder when Fox
Kids got in on the action and launched a Swamp

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:03.449
Thing animated series.

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:07.349
That one only lasted five episodes before
it got axed, but it did give the world the

00:06:07.349 --> 00:06:09.440
gift of an incredibly weird theme song.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:16.460
“Swamp Thing, you are amazing.”

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:18.160
Thanks for watching!

